{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013iceCdv8t1Utgn2puNrXo5","type":"tool_result","content":"\"The chapter opens with the narrator, Montresor, vowing revenge against Fortunato for an unspecified insult, resolved to punish him with impunity during the chaotic carnival season. At dusk, Montresor lures the drunken, costumed Fortunato—wearing a jingling motley outfit and conical cap—through the damp, bone-strewn catacombs beneath his palazzo by feigning concern over a pipe of supposed Amontillado, exploiting Fortunato’s pride in his wine expertise. As they descend deeper into the vaults, Montresor offers wine to mask the rising nitre and feigns concern for Fortunato’s cough, subtly guiding him toward a hidden recess where he chains him to the wall with iron staples. Montresor then methodically walls up the niche with stone and mortar, brick by brick, while Fortunato’s initial laughter turns to desperate pleas and finally silence, leaving the narrator to seal the tomb and restore the bones over it, ensuring no one will disturb the grave for fifty years.\"","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2328c887-9860-4905-a9f6-317b02fbfb9a","uuid":"eba11c13-5c1e-4950-b568-a009b68223f8","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\"The chapter opens with the narrator, Montresor, vowing revenge against Fortunato for an unspecified insult, resolved to punish him with impunity during the chaotic carnival season. At dusk, Montresor lures the drunken, costumed Fortunato—wearing a jingling motley outfit and conical cap—through the damp, bone-strewn catacombs beneath his palazzo by feigning concern over a pipe of supposed Amontillado, exploiting Fortunato’s pride in his wine expertise. As they descend deeper into the vaults, Montresor offers wine to mask the rising nitre and feigns concern for Fortunato’s cough, subtly guiding him toward a hidden recess where he chains him to the wall with iron staples. Montresor then methodically walls up the niche with stone and mortar, brick by brick, while Fortunato’s initial laughter turns to desperate pleas and finally silence, leaving the narrator to seal the tomb and restore the bones over it, ensuring no one will disturb the grave for fifty years.\"","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false}}
