{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017QkqhwcTywuGi5HRN3To9w","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Current Evaluation State\n     2→\n     3→## Active Text\n     4→- **Name:** cask_of_amontillado\n     5→- **Attempt:** 1\n     6→- **Phase:** awaiting_fix\n     7→- **baseline_score:** 6.10\n     8→\n     9→## Latest Scores\n    10→- Structure Detection: 9/10\n    11→- Character Extraction: 4/10 ← CRITICAL FAILURE\n    12→- Character Profiles: 4/10 ← CRITICAL FAILURE\n    13→- Chapter Summaries: 6/10\n    14→- Pronunciation Guide: 7/10\n    15→- HTML Presentation: 8/10\n    16→- **Overall: 6.10/10** (threshold: 8.0)\n    17→\n    18→## Current Issues (Priority Order)\n    19→\n    20→### CRITICAL\n    21→\n    22→1. **\"Amontillado\" (a wine) is listed as a character**\n    23→   - Problem: The analysis created a character entry for \"Amontillado\" which is the Spanish sherry wine, NOT a person\n    24→   - Evidence: `characters[0].canonical_name` = \"Amontillado\" with 16 mentions\n    25→   - The description text is actually describing Montresor (the narrator) but attributed to the wine name\n    26→   - Location: Likely `src/pipeline/character_extraction/` - NER or LLM extraction is treating the title word as a character\n    27→   - Root cause: The word \"Amontillado\" appears frequently in the text (34 times) as part of dialogue about the wine, and the system incorrectly identified it as a character name\n    28→   - Fix approach: Add filtering to exclude common nouns/objects that appear in titles from character extraction, or improve the LLM prompt to distinguish between characters and objects\n    29→\n    30→2. **Montresor NOT identified as narrator**\n    31→   - Problem: `is_narrator: false` for Montresor, but he IS the first-person narrator\n    32→   - Evidence: The story opens with \"I had borne as I best could\" and continues in first person throughout\n    33→   - The `plot_summary` incorrectly calls the narrator